iled description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='136c0bac-1deb-4c2e-84b9-114d9881b991'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out the root cause of the issue with the 'rate' microservice. The user has been going through various steps to diagnose the problem. They started by checking the services, deployments, and pods in the hotel-reservation namespace. The 'rate' pod was found to be in a CrashLoopBackOff state with exit code 2. \n\nFirst, they checked the logs of the 'rate' pod, which showed the container exiting with code 2. Then, they described the pod to get more details, which confirmed the exit code and the restart count. The next step was to check the dependency graph to see if there were any missing dependencies, but that didn't reveal any issues. \n\nThey then queried metrics for container errors, but the result was empty. The user then retrieved the pod's YAML configuration to check the container's settings. The image was 'yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest', and the command was 'rate'. The environment variables included JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO set to 1. \n\nFinally, they used the get_traces tool to look for any traces related to the 'rate' service, but the response was empty. Since all the available tools didn't provide specific error details, the conclusion was that the root cause is an unhandled error in the 'rate' service's application code, possibly due to a missing dependency or misconfiguration. The exit code 2 indicates a command-line error, which might be due to the 'rate' command not being found or a syntax issue. However, since the image is already present and the command is set, the issue might be within the application's logic or environment setup. The user is advised to check the application's code or environment variables for further details.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure in the **'rate' microservice** is an **unhandled error within its application code**, likely due to a **missing dependency or misconfiguration**. The pod repeatedly crashes with **exit code 2**, which typically indicates a command-line syntax error or a missing executable. Despite thorough checks of logs, metrics, dependency graphs, and container configurations, no specific error details were captured. This suggests a potential bug in the service's logic or an unresolved runtime issue. Further investigation into the application's code, environment variables, or dependencies is required to resolve the issue. \n\n**Fault Location**: The 'rate' microservice's application code or its runtime environment.  \n**Cause**: Unhandled error (possibly missing dependency, misconfiguration, or application bug) leading to exit code 2.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e1558b4a-b93a-442a-994d-4d5ce6e4fbcc'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query 