tl_cmd_safely\' can list pods) would help. Wait, the available tools include \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\', so I can use that to run \'kubectl get pods\' in the namespace to get the current pod names.\n\nOnce I have the correct pod name, I can then proceed to check the filesystem. Also, maybe the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, so the name might include a different suffix like \'-1\' or \'-2\'. Alternatively, the pod might not be running, so the name might not be available yet. \n\nSo the next step is to retrieve the list of pods in the hotel-reservation namespace to find the correct name. Then, use that name in subsequent commands. Let me structure the tool call to get the pods.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The pod "hotel-reservation-rate-0" was not found, so I need to first verify the current pod names in the "hotel-reservation" namespace to identify the correct target. This will ensure the subsequent commands use an accurate pod name.\n\ntool_call: \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 417, 'prompt_tokens': 3003, 'total_tokens': 3420, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-a30b-78b3-8ebd-cce3a4066d41-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3003, 'output_tokens': 417, 'total_tokens': 3420}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='aa08d423-0fa9-4455-bf5c-8b48e4f36a33'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to debug the \'rate\' microservice in the hotel-reservation namespace. They\'ve been using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to check if the \'rate\' binary exists in the container, but they\'re running into issues.\n\nFirst, they tried to exec into the pod with the command "kubectl exec -it hotel-reservation-rate-0 -- which rate", but the system rejected the pipe. Then they tried using \'find\' without the pipe, but the interactive flag -it was still causing problems. The next step was to check if the pod actually exists, and when they tried to exec into it, the system said the pod wasn\'t found.\n\nSo now, the user needs to verify the current pod names in the namespace. The correct approach is to first list all the pods in the "hotel-reservation" namespace to find the correct pod name. That way, any subsequent commands can use the accurate pod name. The command "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will retrieve the list of pods, including their names and statuses. Once they have the correct pod name, they can proceed to check the container\'s filesystem without relying on a potentially incorrect pod name like "hotel-reservation-rate-0".\n\nThe user\'s last tool call was to get the pods, which is the right step. The response from the tool will show the actual pod names, which might be different from the assumed "hotel-reservation-rate-0". After obtaining the correct pod name, they can then run the \'find\' command again to check for the \'rate\' binary. This ensures that the subsequent commands are targeting the correct pod, avoiding the "not found" error. If the pod is indeed not found, it might indicate a different issue, like the service not being deployed or the pod being in a terminated state. But for now, verifying the pod names is the logical next step.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bff6ef7c452ecc15', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 436, 'prompt_tokens': 4132, 'total_tokens': 4568, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-b678-78b1-9b3a-e2a9cf0730c4-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bff6ef7c452ecc15', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4132, 'output_tokens': 436, 'total_tokens': 4568}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS             RESTARTS        AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-r62q5                   1/1     Running            0               4m37s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-qh8f9                 1/1     Running            0               4m37s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-x9tvm                      1/1     Running            1 (3m51s ago)   4m37s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-wql2k                   1/1     Running            0               4m36s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-p9924         1/1     Running            0               4m35s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-p7kn9            1/1     Running            0               4m34s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-tvmbc        1/1     Running            0               4m34s\nmongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-k7zc7               1/1     Running            0               4m33s\nmongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-92crx          1/1     Running            0               4m32s\nmongodb-rate-bfbcf4587-km8wz              1/1     Running            0               4m30s\nmongodb-recommendation-674dddbcdf-2f6vv   1/1     Running            0               4m30s\nmongodb-reservation-6cdf69dcc-d6lz9       1/1     Running            0               4m29s\nmongodb-user-6dcf4877-zwbp8               1/1     Running            0               4m27s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-29n75                  1/1     Running            0               4m26s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x                     0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4 (77s ago)     4m25s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-d59jq           1/1     Running            1 (4m5s ago)    4m24s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-2wqgl              1/1     Running            2 (3m46s ago)   4m23s\nsearch-5f668579c9-568th                   1/1     Running            2 (4m8s ago)    4m22s\nuser-6548d868cd-6kzwv                     1/1     Running            1 (4m1s ago)    4m20s\nwrk2-job-ft4js                            1/1     Running            0               4m18s\n', id='63e8be2a-b50b-4599-b912-fa338d179ab7', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bff6ef7c452ecc15'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='02687771-b3cf-4091-a7e5-bf7f3b7971f1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the \'rate\' microservice is crashing. From the previous steps, they tried checking the pod\'s filesystem for the \'rate\' binary but got an error because the pod name was incorrect. Now, they used \'kubectl get pods\' in the hotel-reservation namespace and found that the correct pod name is \'rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x\' instead of \'hotel-reservation-rate-0\'. \n\nSo the next step is to check the \'rate\' pod\'s logs to understand why it\'s crashing. The logs might show the actual error message that caused the exit code 2. The user should use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to run \'kubectl logs\' on the correct pod. This will retrieve the logs and help identify the root cause, like a missing dependency or a configuration error.\n\nThey need to make sure they use the correct pod name this time. The command should be \'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation\' to get the logs. This will provide insights into why the container is exiting with code 2. Once they have the logs, they can determine the next steps, such as checking the application code or environment variables.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The correct pod name for the \'rate\' service is now known (`rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x`). To diagnose the crash, we need to retrieve the container logs to identify the exact error causing exit code 2. This will help determine if it\'s a missing dependency, misconfiguration, or application-level issue.\n\ntool_call: \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 415, 'prompt_tokens': 3922, 'total_tokens': 4337, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-cd97-7481-b9bf-60ea62403a31-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3922, 'output_tokens': 415, 'total_tokens': 4337}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1f7a21d5-1dd1-4811-a955-5443b9760281'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me figure out what to do next. The user is trying to diagnose why the \'rate\' pod is crashing with exit code 2. They\'ve already checked the pod list and found the correct pod name: rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x. The next step was to get the container logs using kubectl logs. But the previous attempt to run that command was rejected because the pod wasn\'t found. Wait, no—the user corrected that by getting the pod list, so now they know the correct pod name.\n\nSo, the current step is to retrieve the logs for the \'rate\' pod. The command they want to run is "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation". But the tool \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' might have restrictions. Let me check the tool\'s description again. The tool says that interactive commands are not supported, like "kubectl edit", and interactive flags like "-it" are not allowed. However, the "kubectl logs" command doesn\'t use interactive flags. The previous error was because of using "-it" in the exec command, which is for interactive shells. But logs don\'t need that. \n\nWait, in the previous attempt, the user tried to run "kubectl exec -it ...", which is why the -it flag was rejected. Now, the command is "kubectl logs", which doesn\'t have interactive flags. So the correct command should be "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation". The tool \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' can handle this as it\'s a non-interactive command. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the logs command. This will retrieve the logs and help identify the root cause of the crash. If the logs show an error like "command not found" for \'rate\', that would confirm the earlier suspicion about the missing binary. Alternatively, other errors in the logs could point to different issues. Once the logs are retrieved, the user can analyze them to proceed with the mitigation.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9beb64130808a337', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 498, 'prompt_tokens': 5087, 'total_tokens': 5585, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-e14a-7a92-9d32-33099baf2e7a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9beb64130808a337', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5087, 'output_tokens': 498, 'total_tokens': 5585}), ToolMessage(content='{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T16:12:27Z","message":"TLS disabled."}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T16:12:27Z","message":"Set global log level: info"}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-17T16:12:27Z","message":"Tune: setGCPercent to 100"}\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T16:12:27Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:24\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mReading config...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T16:12:27Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:37\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mRead database URL: mongodb-rate:27017\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-17T16:12:27Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:38\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mInitializing DB connection...\x1b[0m\n', id='93cd3b73-799c-4ae8-92d6-72b4bd05eefd', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9beb64130808a337'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='85a3a49d-2a36-435f-8a90-79d5a148cedc')]]
2026-03-17 16:12:37 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 5] Inject tool call prompt (repeated) - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_prompt_inject_step:85
2026-03-17 16:12:43 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 5] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='136c0bac-1deb-4c2e-84b9-114d9881b991'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out the root cause of the issue with the 'rate' microservice. The user has been going through various steps to diagnose the problem. They started by checking the services, deployments, and pods in the hotel-reservation namespace. The 'rate' pod was found to be in a CrashLoopBackOff state with exit code 2. \n\nFirst, they checked the logs of the 'rate' pod, which showed the container exiting with code 2. Then, they described the pod to get more details, which confirmed the exit code and the restart count. The next step was to check the dependency graph to see if there were any missing dependencies, but that didn't reveal any issues. \n\nThey then queried metrics for container errors, but the result was empty. The user then retrieved the pod's YAML configuration to check the container's settings. The image was 'yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest', and the command was 'rate'. The environment variables included JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO set to 1. \n\nFinally, they used the get_traces tool to look for any traces related to the 'rate' service, but the response was empty. Since all the available tools didn't provide specific error details, the conclusion was that the root cause is an unhandled error in the 'rate' service's application code, possibly due to a missing dependency or misconfiguration. The exit code 2 indicates a command-line error, which might be due to the 'rate' command not being found or a syntax issue. However, since the image is already present and the command is set, the issue might be within the application's logic or environment setup. The user is advised to check the application's code or environment variables for further details.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure in the **'rate' microservice** is an **unhandled error within its application code**, likely due to a **missing dependency or misconfiguration**. The pod repeatedly crashes with **exit code 2**, which typically indicates a command-line syntax error or a missing executable. Despite thorough checks of logs, metrics, dependency graphs, and container configurations, no specific error details were captured. This suggests a potential bug in the service's logic or an unresolved runtime issue. Further investigation into the application's code, environment variables, or dependencies is required to resolve the issue. \n\n**Fault Location**: The 'rate' microservice's application code or its runtime environment.  \n**Cause**: Unhandled error (possibly missing dependency, misconfiguration, or application bug) leading to exit code 2.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e1558b4a-b93a-442a-994d-4d5ce6e4fbcc'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='72526423-ee1c-4492-9987-9a6161ab454d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to mitigate the fault in the \'rate\' microservice. The previous steps showed that the pod is in CrashLoopBackOff with exit code 2. They checked logs, metrics, dependency graph, and traces but didn\'t find specific errors. Now, the available tools include get_traces, get_services, get_operations, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, exec_kubectl_cmd_safely, wait_tool, and submit_tool.\n\nSince the exit code is 2, which usually indicates a command-line error, maybe the \'rate\' command isn\'t found or there\'s a syntax issue. The user already checked the pod\'s YAML and the image is \'yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\' with the command \'rate\'. So the image might be missing the \'rate\' executable, or there\'s a typo. But how to verify that?\n\nThe exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool can be used to check the container\'s files. For example, checking if the \'rate\' binary exists in the container. Alternatively, maybe the container isn\'t starting because of a missing dependency. But without access to the container\'s filesystem, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nWait, the user can\'t exec into the container directly unless they have the right tools. Since the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely is available, maybe they can run a command like \'ls /path/to/execute/rate\' or check the container\'s processes. But the exact command would depend on the container\'s setup. Alternatively, maybe the container is crashing because the \'rate\' command isn\'t present, so checking the image\'s contents would help. \n\nBut the user might not have the exact path. Alternatively, they could check the pod\'s logs again for more details. However, the previous log check didn\'t show anything. Maybe the logs are not detailed enough. \n\nAnother approach: check the service\'s dependencies. The get_dependency_graph tool could show if any services are missing. But the user already checked that and found no issues. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics to check for any errors or exceptions in the container. But the previous get_metrics call returned empty. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the container\'s environment variables. The pod\'s YAML shows JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO is set to 1. Maybe there\'s a misconfiguration there. But how to check that? \n\nAlternatively, maybe the \'rate\' service is dependent on another service that\'s down. But the dependency graph didn\'t show any issues. \n\nSince the exit code is 2, which is a command error, the most likely cause is that the \'rate\' command isn\'t found in the container. To confirm, the user could exec into the container and check if the \'rate\' binary exists. Using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run a command like \'which rate\' or \'ls /usr/bin/rate\' (assuming