he logs, they can determine the next steps, such as checking the application code or environment variables.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The correct pod name for the \'rate\' service is now known (`rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x`). To diagnose the crash, we need to retrieve the container logs to identify the exact error causing exit code 2. This will help determine if it\'s a missing dependency, misconfiguration, or application-level issue.\n\ntool_call: \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 415, 'prompt_tokens': 3922, 'total_tokens': 4337, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-cd97-7481-b9bf-60ea62403a31-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3922, 'output_tokens': 415, 'total_tokens': 4337}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1f7a21d5-1dd1-4811-a955-5443b9760281'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me figure out what to do next. The user is trying to diagnose why the \'rate\' pod is crashing with exit code 2. They\'ve already checked the pod list and found the correct pod name: rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x. The next step was to get the container logs using kubectl logs. But the previous attempt to run that command was rejected because the pod wasn\'t found. Wait, no—the user corrected that by getting the pod list, so now they know the correct pod name.\n\nSo, the current step is to retrieve the logs for the \'rate\' pod. The command they want to run is "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation". But the tool \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' might have restrictions. Let me check the tool\'s description again. The tool says that interactive commands are not supported, like "kubectl edit", and interactive flags like "-it" are not allowed. However, the "kubectl logs" command doesn\'t use interactive flags. The previous error was because of using "-it" in the exec command, which is for interactive shells. But logs don\'t need that. \n\nWait, in the previous attempt, the user tried to run "kubectl exec -it ...", which is why the -it flag was rejected. Now, the command is "kubectl logs", which doesn\'t have interactive flags. So the correct command should be "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation". The tool \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' can handle this as it\'s a non-interactive command. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the logs command. This will retrieve the logs and help identify the root cause of the crash. If the logs show an error like "command not found" for \'rate\', that would confirm the earlier suspicion about the missing binary. Alternatively, other errors in the logs could point to different issues. Once the logs are retrieved, the user can analyze them to proceed with the mitigation.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-zr48x -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9beb64130808a337', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 498, 'prompt_tokens': 5087, 'total_tokens': 5585, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-e14a-7a92-9d32-33099baf2e7a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'com