ns the necessary Secret for MongoDB credentials isn\'t present.\n\nThe next step is to create a Secret in the \'hotel-reservation\' namespace that contains the MongoDB credentials. The Secret should have keys like MONGO_USER and MONGO_PASSWORD. Then, the \'rate\' deployment needs to be updated to use these environment variables. \n\nBut wait, the user can\'t create a Secret directly through kubectl in the current toolset. However, the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool can be used to apply a Secret YAML. So, the plan is to generate a Secret YAML with the required credentials, apply it using kubectl, and then update the \'rate\' deployment to reference these environment variables. \n\nAlternatively, if the Secret isn\'t created, the \'rate\' service can\'t authenticate, leading to the connection error. So, creating the Secret is crucial. Once the Secret is in place, the \'rate\' deployment should be modified to include the environment variables pointing to the Secret. Then, redeploying the \'rate\' service should allow it to connect to MongoDB with the correct credentials, resolving the issue.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: No MongoDB credentials Secret exists in the namespace, which is required for authentication. I will create a Secret (`mongodb-rate-creds`) with username/password and update the \'rate\' deployment to use these credentials via environment variables. This will resolve the "no reachable servers" error caused by missing authentication.\n\ntool_call: \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: v1\\nkind: Secret\\nmetadata:\\n  name: mongodb-rate-creds\\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\\ntype: Opaque\\nstringData:\\n  MONGO_USER: rate_user\\n  MONGO_PASSWORD: securepassword123\\nEOF"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 565, 'prompt_tokens': 14266