y, use a single command without a pipe. For example, "ls /usr/local/bin | grep rate" can be replaced with "ls /usr/local/bin | grep rate" but that\'s the same. Wait, maybe the tool doesn\'t allow any pipes, so I need to avoid them entirely. So perhaps use \'find\' without a pipe. For example, "find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'" to search for the file. That way, there\'s no pipe, and it\'s a single command.\n\nSo the correct command would be "find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'" to check if the \'rate\' binary exists in that directory. This avoids using a pipe and directly searches for the file. That should work without triggering the pipe command error.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The previous attempt used a pipe (`|`), which is forbidden. To check if the `rate` binary exists in the container\'s filesystem without using pipes, I\'ll use `find` to directly search for the file. This avoids the pipe and confirms whether the executable is present.\n\ntool_call: \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl exec -it hotel-reservation-rate-0 -- find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 567, 'prompt_tokens': 2490, 'total_tokens': 3057, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-58ca-72d3-928b-dd01b1d9844f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2490, 'output_tokens': 567, 'total_tokens': 3057}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ca3c9c73-797c-476e-bc42-ec5484f4cb01'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me think. The user is trying to check if the 'rate' binary exists in the container. They tried using 'ls /usr/local/bin | grep rate' but got an error because pipes are not allowed. So, the next step is to use a command that doesn't involve pipes. The 'find' command is a good alternative because it can search for the file directly without needing a pipe. The command 'find /usr/local/bin -name 'rate'' will look for the 'rate' executable in the /usr/local/bin directory. This should work and avoid the previous error. Let me make sure the command is correctly formatted without any pipes. Yes, using 'find' directly should be acceptable. So the tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command as specified.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl exec -it hotel-reservation-rate-0 -- find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9cc5efb1f6e7ef44', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 215, 'prompt_tokens': 3638, 'total_tokens': 3853, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc91-72ec-7960-a4c3-8a7ebebf79bc-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': "kubectl exec -it hotel-reservation-rate-0 -- find /usr/local/bin -name 'rate'"}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9cc5efb1f6e7ef44', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3638, 'output_tokens': 215, 'total_tokens': 3853}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected (ValueError): Interactive flag detected: -it. Such commands are not supported. Try to use the command non-interactively.', id='8f3269e8-1d76-48ee-bee0-0bc937a6b72e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9cc5efb1f6e7ef44'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='22be1c29-32d3-4510-8557-9ddddb51f9bf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user is trying to check if the \'rate\' binary exists in the container. They tried using `find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'` with `kubectl exec` but got rejected because of the `-it` flag, which is interactive and not allowed. So, I need to adjust the command to be non-interactive.\n\nFirst, I should remove the `-it` flag from the `kubectl exec` command. That way, it won\'t require an interactive terminal. The command should still run the `find` command inside the container. \n\nWait, the previous attempt used `kubectl exec -it ...`, which is why it was rejected. The correct approach is to use `kubectl exec` without the `-it` options. So the command should be `kubectl exec hotel-reservation-rate-0 -- find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'`. \n\nThis way, it\'s a non-interactive command, which is allowed. The tool will execute the `find` command inside the pod\'s container to check for the presence of the \'rate\' binary. If the file exists, the command will return its path; otherwise, it\'ll return nothing. This helps determine if the missing executable is the root cause of the exit code 2.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The `-it` flag is interactive and not supported. To check for the existence of the `rate` binary without requiring an interactive shell, I\'ll run a non-interactive `find` command to search for the file in the container\'s filesystem. This avoids the pipe and interactive flags while achieving the same goal.\n\ntool_call: \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl exec hotel-reservation-rate-0 -- find /usr/local/bin -name \'rate\'"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 389, 'prompt_tokens': 2746, 'total_token